      "name": "Mullein Oil",
      "cat": "infused-oils",
      "price": 18,
      "compareAtPrice": 0,
      "desc": "Mullein Infused Oil has been used for centuries as a remedy for earaches, eczema, rheumatic joints and many other skin conditions.\n\nMullein leaves and flowers are considered expectorants (promotes the discharge of mucus) and demulcents (soothes irritated mucous membranes). Mullein has been used by many as a remedy for the respiratory tract, Especially in the case of irritating coughs with bronchial congestion. Due to its mucilage content, mullein has also been used topically by many as a soothing emollient for inflammatory skin conditions and burns.\n\nDid you know that study even showed a benefit for ear pain based on a trial of 171 children between 5 and 18 years old with an ear infection. But always used with caution for ear infections , some naturopathic doctors apply an oil extract directly in the ear but if the eardrum has ruptured, nothing should be put directly in the ear. So also ways consult your physician before considering an herbal remedy for examination in the case of an ear drum possibly being ruptured.\n\nWhen used typically mullein is applied to the skin for wounds, burns, hemorrhoids, bruises, frostbite, and skin infections (cellulitis). The leaves are used topically to soften and protect the skin when turned into a salve, poultice, and even as a compress.\n\nThese statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. This product is not?intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.\n\n\nHerbalistic Wellness is a proud supporter of the National Multiple Sclerosis Society Visit them today to donate @ www.nationalmssociety.org",
      "fullDesc": "Made the slow, old-fashioned way. Fresh organic mullein blossoms and organic garlic are gently infused in pure olive oil under the summer sun, then strained and bottled in small batches. It's a golden, fragrant oil from a folk recipe that home herbalists have passed down for generations. Warm a small amount between your palms and massage gently around the outer ear and along the jawline — the warmth and the slow massage are the whole ritual. For external use only. If you have ear concerns, talk with your healthcare provider first.",
